<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marc-Antoine Charpentier</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1643">1643</a>–<a href="/years/1704">1704</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Marc-Antoine Charpentier was a French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> composer under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>, born in or near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, whose sacred vocal music was especially admired in his lifetime. After studying in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> with Giacomo Carissimi, probably between 1667 and 1669, he was for seventeen years house composer to Marie de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>, duchesse de Guise, writing Italianate oratorios and motets, and wrote music for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moliere">Molière</a>'s The Imaginary Invalid. From late 1687 he was music master to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> in Paris, and from 1698 until his death in 1704 maître de musique of the Sainte-Chapelle. More than 500 of his works survive in 28 autograph volumes, and the prelude of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/te-deum">Te Deum</a> H.146 has opened Eurovision broadcasts since 1954.</p></div>
